WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.451
This is Chapter 2: emergency
access accounts as code.

4
00:00:09.451 --> 00:00:13.756
These accounts are the one standing
privilege the program keeps on purpose,

5
00:00:13.756 --> 00:00:16.627
so the chapter checks them
on every run and watches

6
00:00:16.627 --> 00:00:19.225
them every minute, with
code someone reviewed.

7
00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:24.136
We'll start from Microsoft's own
emergency access page, see what its sample

8
00:00:24.136 --> 00:00:28.563
alert misses, then walk the check,
the alert, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:28.563 --> 00:00:31.773
One note before we begin:
the code is illustrative,

10
00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:35.410
tested against fixtures
rather than a live tenant.

11
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.198
Why start here?

12
00:00:40.198 --> 00:00:41.810
Because the order matters.

13
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:47.214
If approval for privileged roles is switched
on before working emergency accounts

14
00:00:47.214 --> 00:00:51.692
exist, you can lock everyone out:
every administrator role is eligible,

15
00:00:51.692 --> 00:00:54.935
approval is required, and
nobody is left to approve.

16
00:00:54.935 --> 00:00:59.142
That's the lockout Microsoft Learn
documents, and it's why the workbook's

17
00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:02.922
privileged-access design makes
this step the one that can't move.

18
00:01:02.922 --> 00:01:05.779
The emergency accounts
are also the program's

19
00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:08.260
main exception to zero standing access.

20
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.829
The inventory in Chapter
1 lets them through

21
00:01:10.829 --> 00:01:13.260
only because they're listed as exceptions.

22
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.200
This chapter makes sure
that exception is real.

23
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.313
Next, which cloud.

24
00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:25.238
The check reads Microsoft Graph,
and the host depends on the tenant.

25
00:01:25.238 --> 00:01:29.912
For a GCC tenant like this estate's,
that's the worldwide Graph endpoint,

26
00:01:29.912 --> 00:01:34.263
and the alert sits in Azure Monitor
in a commercial subscription.

27
00:01:34.263 --> 00:01:36.784
The template the code
writes names no host,

28
00:01:36.784 --> 00:01:40.458
so the same file could deploy in
Azure Government for GCC High,

29
00:01:40.458 --> 00:01:44.925
although the chapter flags that full
availability there wasn't confirmed.

30
00:01:44.925 --> 00:01:49.202
And the two sign-in methods Learn
wants for these accounts, passkeys and

31
00:01:49.202 --> 00:01:54.280
certificate-based authentication, are
available in every one of these clouds.

32
00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.742
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

33
00:01:59.742 --> 00:02:02.441
Learn's emergency access
page is the standard:

34
00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:05.921
at least two cloud-only accounts
on the onmicrosoft domain,

35
00:02:05.921 --> 00:02:10.538
strong phishing-resistant sign-in that's
different from your everyday admins,

36
00:02:10.538 --> 00:02:12.953
Global Administrator held permanently,

37
00:02:12.953 --> 00:02:16.930
and exclusion from blocking policies
through one dedicated group.

38
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.474
Then you have to run
them: watch their logs,

39
00:02:19.474 --> 00:02:21.943
and test them at least every ninety days.

40
00:02:21.943 --> 00:02:26.615
The workbook adopts Learn's standard
and adds two things of its own:

41
00:02:26.615 --> 00:02:29.860
dual custody, and a
review after every use.

42
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:38.315
Learn also gives you a sample alert:
three short queries over the sign-in logs,

43
00:02:38.315 --> 00:02:42.647
and portal steps to turn them into an
alert rule where any result fires.

44
00:02:42.647 --> 00:02:45.483
Those queries only exist
inside the web page,

45
00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:50.309
so the repository pins the page's source,
unmodified, with a hash a test checks,

46
00:02:50.309 --> 00:02:53.986
and a weekly watch that reports
when Microsoft changes it.

47
00:02:53.986 --> 00:02:58.973
A small harness then runs the sample's
filters over a handful of fictional log rows,

48
00:02:58.973 --> 00:03:02.860
so you can see exactly what the
sample catches and what it misses.

49
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.370
It's a test, not a query engine.

50
00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.882
This is the heart of the chapter.

51
00:03:10.882 --> 00:03:13.738
Run as published, the
sample watches only one of

52
00:03:13.738 --> 00:03:16.595
the three places an
emergency account shows up.

53
00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:20.522
It misses the non-interactive sign-in,
which is exactly what a stolen

54
00:03:20.522 --> 00:03:24.870
session looks like, and Learn notes that
passkey sign-ins now land there too.

55
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:29.708
It misses the audit events, like a new
method quietly registered on the account.

56
00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:33.194
And if you copy it with its
placeholders, it alerts on nothing.

57
00:03:33.194 --> 00:03:36.695
So the verdict is: keep
Learn's object-ID query and its

58
00:03:36.695 --> 00:03:40.710
alert settings as the core,
and let the code add the rest.

59
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:45.785
So what does the code add?

60
00:03:45.785 --> 00:03:50.248
Four commands and an entry script,
added to the module from Chapter 1.

61
00:03:50.248 --> 00:03:52.636
One checks the accounts
against the standard.

62
00:03:52.636 --> 00:03:55.697
One writes the alert query
from the account IDs.

63
00:03:55.697 --> 00:03:59.797
One wraps that query in a deployable
template, without deploying it.

64
00:03:59.797 --> 00:04:05.310
And one runs the same match over exported
rows, so you can review a drill offline.

65
00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.279
Two small files drive it: one
names the accounts and their

66
00:04:09.279 --> 00:04:13.248
exclusion group by object ID,
and one records every drill.

67
00:04:13.248 --> 00:04:17.370
It reuses Chapter 1's reader,
so it only ever reads.

68
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.095
Here are the ten rules the
check applies on every run.

69
00:04:24.095 --> 00:04:28.691
Most come straight from Learn's standard:
enough accounts, the right domain,

70
00:04:28.691 --> 00:04:32.694
cloud-only, Global Administrator
held permanently and directly,

71
00:04:32.694 --> 00:04:36.400
the right sign-in methods,
membership in the exclusion group,

72
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.995
and a passing drill in
the last ninety days.

73
00:04:38.995 --> 00:04:42.670
Two are the program's own design,
and they're labeled that way:

74
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:47.695
a disabled emergency account is a finding,
and so is anyone in the exclusion group

75
00:04:47.695 --> 00:04:52.720
who isn't an emergency account, because
everyone in that group skips every policy.

76
00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.160
The full wording is on the slide.

77
00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.660
One rule needs a closer look.

78
00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.465
Learn says to exclude
the emergency accounts

79
00:05:03.465 --> 00:05:06.197
from policies that block
or restrict sign-in.

80
00:05:06.197 --> 00:05:08.953
Deciding which policies
do that is a judgment,

81
00:05:08.953 --> 00:05:13.810
and the code shouldn't make judgments, so
it checks every enforced policy instead.

82
00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.426
The sources also disagree in two places,

83
00:05:16.426 --> 00:05:21.023
and the chapter records both so nobody
quietly loosens the code later.

84
00:05:21.023 --> 00:05:25.260
Report-only policies are reported
as information, not failures.

85
00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.844
And whether the accounts belong to
individuals is something Graph can't see,

86
00:05:29.844 --> 00:05:35.020
so the program keeps the workbook's design:
the accounts aren't tied to one person.

87
00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:39.808
Now the alert.

88
00:05:39.808 --> 00:05:44.478
The query has three legs: interactive
sign-ins, non-interactive sign-ins,

89
00:05:44.478 --> 00:05:49.069
and audit events where an emergency
account is the actor or the target.

90
00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:53.693
It deliberately doesn't use a fuzzy
union, because if a table never arrives,

91
00:05:53.693 --> 00:05:57.482
a fuzzy union returns nothing,
and silence looks like safety.

92
00:05:57.482 --> 00:06:01.968
The template keeps Learn's settings,
severity zero and any result fires,

93
00:06:01.968 --> 00:06:05.845
and checks that it points at a
real workspace and action group.

94
00:06:05.845 --> 00:06:09.345
It deploys nothing; that's
the reviewed pipeline's job.

95
00:06:09.345 --> 00:06:11.590
And remember the two prerequisites:

96
00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:15.240
the logs have to be sent, and
the license has to be there.

97
00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.052
Who is this for?

98
00:06:20.052 --> 00:06:21.091
Three groups.

99
00:06:21.091 --> 00:06:22.477
Developers build it.

100
00:06:22.477 --> 00:06:24.994
The identity engineers own the accounts,

101
00:06:24.994 --> 00:06:28.028
the exclusion group and
the record of every drill.

102
00:06:28.028 --> 00:06:30.603
And security operations receive the alert,

103
00:06:30.603 --> 00:06:33.178
and hear about each
drill before it happens.

104
00:06:33.178 --> 00:06:35.787
The reader app gets six Graph permissions,

105
00:06:35.787 --> 00:06:39.215
each the least privileged for
its call, and nothing more.

106
00:06:39.215 --> 00:06:41.447
Two of those choices are worth noticing:

107
00:06:41.447 --> 00:06:45.641
reading whether an account is enabled
or synchronized needs the full user

108
00:06:45.641 --> 00:06:49.940
read permission, because those
properties aren't in the basic profile.

109
00:06:53.690 --> 00:07:00.002
Lab 1 needs nothing but a laptop: PowerShell
7 and Pester, no tenant and no network.

110
00:07:00.002 --> 00:07:03.717
First you run Learn's own queries
over the fictional export,

111
00:07:03.717 --> 00:07:07.433
and the tests show what you'd
otherwise have to take on trust:

112
00:07:07.433 --> 00:07:10.314
of five emergency events,
the sample finds two.

113
00:07:10.314 --> 00:07:14.753
Then you list the six permissions, and
run the check against a fictional tenant.

114
00:07:14.753 --> 00:07:18.878
It reports nine findings, no
warnings and one informational item,

115
00:07:18.878 --> 00:07:21.628
and it writes the alert
template for review.

116
00:07:21.628 --> 00:07:24.250
Next, let's read those findings.

117
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.025
Reading the findings is
where the rules become real.

118
00:07:31.025 --> 00:07:33.025
One fictional account passes.

119
00:07:33.025 --> 00:07:35.195
The other fails five different ways,

120
00:07:35.195 --> 00:07:39.463
from the wrong domain to an expiring
Global Administrator assignment.

121
00:07:39.463 --> 00:07:42.367
Three enforced policies
don't exclude the accounts,

122
00:07:42.367 --> 00:07:46.150
and someone who shouldn't be there
is sitting in the exclusion group.

123
00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.873
Then the offline match finds
all five emergency events,

124
00:07:49.873 --> 00:07:54.162
where the sample found two, and
ignores an ordinary user's sign-in.

125
00:07:54.162 --> 00:07:58.625
Finally you try to feed it a user name
instead of an ID, and it refuses.

126
00:07:58.625 --> 00:08:02.470
Run the whole suite, and three
hundred thirty tests pass.

127
00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.470
Lab 2 moves to a test
tenant, never production.

128
00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.695
You send the three log
categories to a test workspace,

129
00:08:12.695 --> 00:08:17.607
register a reader app with exactly the six
permissions, and run the check for real.

130
00:08:17.607 --> 00:08:22.057
Then the important step: paste the query
into the workspace and run it by hand,

131
00:08:22.057 --> 00:08:26.083
because an error that names a table
tells you the logs aren't arriving.

132
00:08:26.083 --> 00:08:28.657
After that you deploy
the reviewed template,

133
00:08:28.657 --> 00:08:32.483
sign in once with a test emergency
account, and watch the alert land.

134
00:08:32.483 --> 00:08:35.969
Record the drill, and the
ninety-day finding clears.

135
00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:37.810
Then back everything out.

136
00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.023
Every one of these dangers has happened
somewhere, and each has a guard in the code.

137
00:08:46.023 --> 00:08:48.942
The worst is the lockout,
and the guard is simply

138
00:08:48.942 --> 00:08:51.711
that this check is the
gate's test, on every run.

139
00:08:51.711 --> 00:08:55.920
The quietest is a fuzzy query
over a table that never arrives:

140
00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.035
nothing fires, and
silence looks like safety.

141
00:08:59.035 --> 00:09:04.485
Others are slower: an assignment that
expires, a phone method tied to one person,

142
00:09:04.485 --> 00:09:08.510
an exclusion group that grows,
or a drill nobody wrote down.

143
00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:09.647
Notice the pattern.

144
00:09:09.647 --> 00:09:14.430
Each guard is a rule that runs every
time, not a person remembering.

145
00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:19.530
Here's what done looks like.

146
00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:23.741
On the account side: at least two
accounts named by ID, no findings,

147
00:09:23.741 --> 00:09:28.508
every enforced policy excluding a
group that holds only those accounts,

148
00:09:28.508 --> 00:09:31.368
and a recorded drill in
the last ninety days.

149
00:09:31.368 --> 00:09:34.839
On the alert side: the logs
arrive, the query runs cleanly,

150
00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:39.517
the deployed rule matches the reviewed
template, the reader holds exactly

151
00:09:39.517 --> 00:09:43.968
six permissions, and the pinned Learn
page is still the one you reviewed.

152
00:09:43.968 --> 00:09:46.108
With the emergency accounts proven,

153
00:09:46.108 --> 00:09:50.105
Chapter 3 can safely build
Conditional Access for administrators.

154
00:09:50.105 --> 00:09:51.270
Thanks for watching.
